    {
      "@id": "experiment_main",
      "@type": "Experiment",
      "mls:description": "Comparison of E2E approach vs Two-step approach on DATA2 dataset.",
      "start_char_doc": 1120,
      "end_char_doc": 1132,
      "wasDerivedFrom": "paper",
      "mls:realizes": { "@id": "task_ner_speech" },
      "mls:produces": { "@id": "model_e2e_ner" },
      "mls:usesData": { "@id": "dataset_data2" },
      "mls:usesParameters": [
        { "@id": "hp_alpha" },
        { "@id": "hp_beta" },
        { "@id": "hp_beam_width" }
      ],
      "context": {
        "prefix": "Named entity recognition (NER) from text has been a widely studied problem and usually extracts semantic information from text. Until now, NER from speech is mostly studied in a two-step pipeline process that includes first applying an automatic speech recognition (ASR) system on an audio sample and then passing the predicted transcript to a NER tagger. In such cases, the error does not propagate from one step to another as both the tasks are not optimized in an end-to-end (E2E) fashion. Recent studies confirm that integrated approaches (e.g., E2E ASR) outperform sequential ones (e.g., p",
        "suffix": "results show that the proposed E2E approach outperforms the classical two-step approach."
      }
    },
    {
